1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,677
We are now
at the exact
point where the
sea meets the
river, one
of the most
significant

2
00:00:05,677 --> 00:00:10,774
moments on the
entire route
through the
Grotta del Bue
Marino. If you
look down

3
00:00:10,774 --> 00:00:16,516
beneath you, you
will notice
a small
difference
in level:
a natural rocky
step just a few

4
00:00:16,516 --> 00:00:21,742
centimetres
high, but enough
to separate two
different
worlds. In the
lower level

5
00:00:21,742 --> 00:00:27,161
is salt water,
coming from the
sea. In the
slightly higher
level is fresh
water, fed

6
00:00:27,161 --> 00:00:32,709
by the
underground
river. During
the summer
months, when
rainfall
is scarce, the
river

7
00:00:32,709 --> 00:00:38,386
significantly
reduces its flow
and tends
to stagnate.
In these
conditions the
two bodies

8
00:00:38,386 --> 00:00:43,676
of water remain
separate,
maintaining
an almost
motionless
equilibrium.
During the

9
00:00:43,676 --> 00:00:48,773
winter months,
on the other
hand, the river
is fed once
again by rain.
The flow

10
00:00:48,773 --> 00:00:54,515
increases and,
already in the
low season,
if you are
lucky, it
is right here
at this spot

11
00:00:54,515 --> 00:00:59,805
that you may
observe a small
waterfall
of fresh water
pouring down
towards the sea

12
00:00:59,805 --> 00:01:05,224
level. It is
a particularly
evocative
moment: before
our very eyes,
the process that

13
00:01:05,224 --> 00:01:11,030
over millennia
has contributed
to the formation
of these
cavities is made
clearly visible.

14
00:01:11,030 --> 00:01:16,514
Here we are not
merely
witnessing the
meeting of two
bodies of water,
but the meeting

15
00:01:16,514 --> 00:01:21,933
of two forces:
the marine and
the fluvial.
It is a dynamic
equilibrium,
which varies

16
00:01:21,933 --> 00:01:27,417
with the seasons
and which
continues, even
today, to slowly
shape the
interior of the

17
00:01:27,417 --> 00:01:28,000
mountain.
